0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "298016d0-4ea6-54d8-a832-231f3f8e4443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419943 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 2,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}(1-\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={(t-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}, & 0\\le x\\le 2 \\\\ 1, & x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126177" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419944 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{225}{(x+15)^{2}}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X\\le 5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3191d215-7e03-5aae-a424-72356e84e8e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419944 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =F(5) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{225}{(5+15)^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419945 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419945 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89700" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419945 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419945 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-x}, & x\\ge 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(2<X\\le 4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3ffe741c-4355-5cb4-a113-2fae2e76fdee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419945 #edb/ref 1074 "We can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2<X\\le 4) & =P(X\\le 4)-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =F(4)-F(2) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-3}-(1-{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-{e}^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89747" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419946 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{21}, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(3<X<4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "502fa5ed-8192-5239-85ce-b51fd4a6a69d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419946 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<X<4) & =P(3<X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 4)-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =F(4)-F(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{4}^{2}-4}{21}-\\frac{{3}^{2}-4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419947 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419947 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419947 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419947 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6056a54f-50c5-51bf-9c90-30d0151550be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419947 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{6}{{t}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{{t}^{6}}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419948 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419948 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419948 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419948 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ 1-{e}^{-6x}, & x\\ge 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(5\\le X\\le 8).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83a3d348-6693-5dcc-af5c-61cd638a0bb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419948 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5\\le X\\le 8) & =P(5<X\\le 8) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 8)-P(X\\le 5) \\\\  & =F(8)-F(5) \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-48}-(1-{e}^{-30}) \\\\  & ={e}^{-30}-{e}^{-48}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419949 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419949 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419949 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419949 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{15}(6-x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9853f7b8-7c5d-5c25-8113-542fa8b8a6a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419949 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{15}(6-t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{15}t(t-12)|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{15}x(x-12)-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+12x-20}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{-{x}^{2}+12x-20}{15}, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{\\pi (1+{e}^{2x})},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa0570cf-0a8d-5b19-8cfc-d1d338d11235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419950 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\pi (1+{e}^{2t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{t}}{\\pi }|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419951 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419951 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419951 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419951 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{(1+{e}^{x})^{3}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419951 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ae38bc39-2bd5-58c3-af2b-ad65de482c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419951 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{(1+{e}^{t})^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{t})^{2}}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89641" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419952 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8{x}^{4}},\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b199a74a-63a2-5637-aa94-6d70ba2bd918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419952 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge \\frac{1}{2},$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x}\\frac{3}{8{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{8{t}^{3}}|}_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{8{x}^{3}}, & x\\ge \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419953 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419953 #edb/ref 1069 "q-89546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419953 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419953 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419953 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d87af21c-48b4-5612-ae7a-18902b89c926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419953 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419954 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{60}{x}^{3},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8d29abd-ea88-5ad4-8655-fbf6ab67a44c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419954 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 4,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{1}{60}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{{t}^{4}}{240}|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{240}-\\frac{1}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}}{240}-\\frac{1}{15}, & 2\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1, & x>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{108}x(x+2),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dad3449e-8974-53d7-9ad6-1544954b39d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419955 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0\\le x\\le 6,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{108}t(t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{324}{t}^{2}(t+3)|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{3}}{324}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{108}, & 0\\le x\\le 6 \\\\ 1, & x>6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419956 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{(1+{e}^{2x})^{3}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dbdaccde-7824-5d29-93f1-f05b495a99f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419956 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{4{e}^{2t}}{(1+{e}^{2t})^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{2t})^{2}}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{2x})^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{2x})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419957 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419957 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126176" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419957 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419957 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<4 \\\\ 1-\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\ge 4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind $P(X>6).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419957 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de421a60-85ed-5a5a-9a98-5df02c4cb3f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419957 #edb/ref 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x).\n$$\n\nHence, the required probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & =1-F(6) \\\\  & =1-(1-\\frac{16}{{6}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186419942 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-210762" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419958 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{e}^{-|4x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb64e84b-4036-56ed-b5be-3b99c498406f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186419958 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\le 0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t, & x>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals. \n\n- If $x\\le 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{4t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn particular, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-4t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2{e}^{4t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}2{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{2},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-4x}}{2},\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
